BEGIN;
DO $$
DECLARE
  additions jsonb := '{"topic-7":"<h4>From a client question to a financial plan</h4><p>Start by determining what the client is trying to accomplish and which facts could change the answer. Collect both numbers and preferences. Agree on the scope, choose goals with the client and compare their current approach with realistic alternatives. Then explain the recommendation, assign implementation responsibilities and agree how changes will trigger a review.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An owner wants $500,000 of life coverage because the company owes $500,000. Verify who owes the debt, personal guarantees, existing coverage and the buy-sell agreement. The family also needs $700,000 to replace income and has $200,000 payable to the family. If the debt and income needs are independent, the initial gap is $500,000 + ($700,000 − $200,000) = $1 million. That total does not tell you how to structure each benefit. The intended recipients and contract terms determine who can use each dollar.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>What changes if the lender can claim the existing $200,000 policy and the family cannot? Assume it applies fully to the debt.</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>Business gap drops to $300,000 and family gap rises to $700,000. The total stays $1 million because the same existing coverage moved between two independent needs.</p></details>","topic-6":"<h4>Evaluate a recommendation and manage its conflict</h4><p>Assess the client’s need before comparing products or accounts. Write down the current arrangement and reasonable alternatives. Compare total costs, liquidity, risk, service and benefits the client would gain or give up. If your compensation changes, explain the conflict in terms the client can understand and document how it is managed.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A client with $400,000 in a former employer plan is considering an IRA. Suppose the plan costs 0.25% a year and the IRA costs 0.85% all-in. The difference is 0.60% × $400,000 = $2,400 a year before growth or balance changes. The higher cost is a fact to weigh against additional services, investment options and lost plan features. Ask whether the client needs near-term withdrawals, then compare the options in writing.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>If the IRA proposal drops to 0.50%, what is the annual cost difference? What fact would still matter?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>$1,000 on the stated balance. Eligibility, plan features, services and liquidity still matter. Cost is one part of the recommendation.</p></details>","topic-12":"<h4>Put competing goals in the same dollars</h4><p>Use present value to compare amounts due on different dates. Draw a timeline, mark today as year zero, and identify whether deposits occur at the beginning or end of each period. Use one consistent period for the rate and number of periods. A future bill is divided by its growth factor to find the deposit today.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Tuition is $30,000 due in five years. At a hypothetical 4%, the deposit today is $30,000 ÷ 1.04⁵ ≈ $24,658. At 2%, it is about $27,172. Lower assumed returns require more money up front. If $18,000 already sits in a separate education account, the remaining today-dollar gap at 4% is about $6,658. Do not use the same cash to fund tuition and an emergency reserve.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>At 4%, what is the present value of $30,000 due in three years, and why does it increase?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>About $26,670. With only three years to earn returns, a larger deposit is needed today.</p></details>","topic-25":"<h4>Build an insurance needs analysis</h4><p>Identify the risks the household can absorb and the ones that could cause lasting financial damage. Separate immediate obligations, future income needs and existing resources. Use assets only if they are accessible and earmarked for the same goal. Check policy duration, owners, beneficiaries, employer dependence and exclusions before recommending coverage.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A household requires $620,000 of survivor income in present-value terms, $185,000 to cover debts and $25,000 for transition costs. It can allocate $80,000 of liquid savings and has $150,000 of group life. The initial gap is $830,000 − $80,000 − $150,000 = $600,000. If the worker leaves and loses the group policy, the gap becomes $750,000. Compare both scenarios and check portability before relying on group coverage.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>How do the gaps change if only $30,000 of the savings can be used because the remaining $50,000 is an emergency reserve?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>The gaps increase by $50,000 to $650,000 with the group policy and $800,000 without it.</p></details>","topic-31":"<h4>Translate a target allocation into actual trades</h4><p>Start with goals, investment horizon, spending needs and the client’s ability to bear losses. Translate that into a target mix. Calculate the total market value before assigning target dollars to each sleeve. Review taxes, transaction costs and whether new cash or tax-deferred accounts can help bring the mix back in line.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A $600,000 portfolio targets 60% stock and 40% bonds. It holds $405,000 stock and $195,000 bonds. The target is $360,000 stock and $240,000 bonds. That is a $45,000 shift from stocks to bonds. A taxable sale can realize gains. If the household has suitable holdings in a traditional IRA, a trade inside it can rebalance without a distribution, while keeping the household-level allocation in view.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>If the client adds $30,000 and invests all of it in bonds, how much still needs to shift from stocks to bonds?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>The new total is $630,000 and the stock target is $378,000. Shift $27,000 out of stock. The bond balance after the contribution and trade is $252,000.</p></details>","topic-37":"<h4>Calculate incremental tax by filling brackets</h4><p>Separate the tax on existing income from the additional tax caused by a decision. Start with projected taxable income, identify how much room remains in each bracket, and apply each rate only to the income in that band. Then check interactions excluded by the simplified model, such as credits and income-related Medicare costs.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Illustrative brackets only. A couple has $86,000 of taxable income. Incremental dollars up to $100,000 are taxed at 12% and the next band at 22%. A $24,000 Roth conversion fills $14,000 at 12% and $10,000 at 22%. Incremental tax is $1,680 + $2,200 = $3,880. Compare the cost today against expected future tax, available cash to pay it and other affected benefits.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>What is the incremental tax for a $14,000 conversion under the same assumptions? How much extra tax does the larger conversion cause?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>$1,680 on the smaller conversion. The extra $10,000 converted in the larger scenario costs $2,200 at the assumed 22% rate.</p></details>","topic-52":"<h4>Build and stress-test a retirement income plan</h4><p>Estimate spending including taxes, subtract reliable income and calculate the draw the portfolio must support. Divide the gap by investable assets to find the initial withdrawal rate. Recalculate after a shock and develop a response the household can actually follow. Include inflation, survivor income and long-term care in the longer model.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A couple expects $90,000 of annual spending and $54,000 of reliable income. A $900,000 portfolio covers the $36,000 gap at a 4% initial draw. If the portfolio drops 20% before the first withdrawal, it falls to $720,000. The same $36,000 now represents 5%. Reducing discretionary spending by $7,200 lowers the draw to $28,800, or 4% of the reduced portfolio.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>If reliable income also drops by $6,000, what spending reduction would restore a 4% draw on $720,000?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>The new gap is $42,000. A 4% draw provides $28,800, so spending must drop by $13,200, assuming no other change.</p></details>","topic-54":"<h4>Trace where assets go and who can use them</h4><p>For each asset, identify legal owner, titling, designated recipient and when the recipient can access the money. Compare the actual forms with stated intent. Estate liquidity is the cash available in time to pay immediate obligations. Probate treatment and estate tax inclusion are separate questions. Bring conflicting documents or uncertain rights to counsel.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An estate needs $140,000 promptly. It has $60,000 of accessible cash, an individually titled business interest and a $300,000 policy naming an adult child. The starting liquidity gap is $80,000. The policy can support the child, but paying the child does not itself put cash into the estate. Assess arrangements for legitimate funding without assuming any beneficiary will volunteer money.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>If a separate account provides another $55,000 that the estate can access by the due date, what remains to be funded?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>$25,000. Count the account only after checking who has authority to access it and when it will be available.</p></details>","topic-69":"<h4>Teach back the real trade-off</h4><p>Share a recommendation in plain language, then ask the client to explain what they understand about the costs, benefits and what could change. Listen for a mistaken expectation rather than asking for a simple yes. Clarify the misunderstanding, document the decision and agree on a follow-up.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An owner wants higher investment returns while holding enough to make a down payment next year. Explain that taking more market risk can jeopardize money needed on a specific date. Separate the amount needed for purchase from money with a longer horizon. Ask the client to describe what would happen if the market fell in the month before closing.</p><h4>Try it yourself</h4><p>What teach-back question would tell you whether the client understands the account’s purpose?</p><details><summary>Reveal the solution</summary><p>“If markets drop before closing, which dollars will be available for the down payment, and which dollars can we leave invested?” A strong response identifies the source, timing and risk of each pool.</p></details>","start":"<h4>Guided learning sequence</h4><p>Start with a worked example, then solve the variation before revealing the answer. Write out the calculation or client decision and explain how one change in facts would alter it. Use the short knowledge checks afterward to confirm recall.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#topic-7\">From a client question to a financial plan</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-6\">Evaluate a recommendation and manage its conflict</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-12\">Put competing goals in the same dollars</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-25\">Build an insurance needs analysis</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-31\">Translate a target allocation into actual trades</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-37\">Calculate incremental tax by filling brackets</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-52\">Build and stress-test a retirement income plan</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-54\">Trace where assets go and who can use them</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-69\">Teach back the real trade-off</a></li></ol>"}'::jsonb;
  existing jsonb;
BEGIN
  SELECT lessons INTO STRICT existing FROM public.tac_academy_courses
    WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep' FOR UPDATE;
  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_object_keys(additions) AS expected(id)
    WHERE (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) AS item WHERE item->>'id' = expected.id) <> 1
  ) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Guided teaching lesson targets do not match';
  END IF;
  UPDATE public.tac_academy_courses
    SET lessons = (
      SELECT jsonb_agg(CASE WHEN additions ? (item->>'id') AND strpos(item->>'html', additions->>(item->>'id')) = 0
        THEN jsonb_set(item, '{html}', to_jsonb((item->>'html') || (additions->>(item->>'id'))))
        ELSE item END ORDER BY ord)
      FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) WITH ORDINALITY AS lesson(item, ord)
    )
    WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep';
END;
$$;
COMMIT;
